{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>who dwells in Heshbon</b> — for it was not his, but he had captured it from the king of Moab and rebuilt it to be his royal seat. And likewise <b>who dwells in Ashtaroth in Edrei</b> — for it was the land of the Rephaim and he was king of the Amorite (Deuteronomy 3:8), as I shall explain with HaShem's help (below, 2:10–11; 3:11). And since he says \"who dwells in Ashtaroth in Edrei,\" it indicates that Ashtaroth is the name of a place in which is Edrei. And thus the language of the Sifrei (Deuteronomy 4): \"in Ashtaroth — for he was as harsh as Ashtaroth; in Edrei — this is the place of kingship.\"\n\nAnd Rashi wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 1:4): <b>Ashtaroth</b> — an expression of crags and hardness, and Ashtaroth is Ashtaroth-karnayim, for there were Rephaim there, as it is stated (Genesis 14:5), \"and they struck the Rephaim in Ashtaroth-karnayim.\" <b>In Edrei</b> — the name of the kingdom, and thus it is written, <b>ashtaroth of your flocks</b> (Deuteronomy 7:13), as its Targum renders. And our Rabbis said (Ḥullin 84a): Why is their name called Ashtaroth? Because they enrich (מְעַשְּׁרוֹת) their owners and strengthen them — like those ashtaroth, which are strong rocks.\n\nAnd the Rav combines in it two expressions, and we do not know from where he derived that <b>ashtaroth</b> should be a term for crags and rocks. But it is possible to say that it is the name for flocks of sheep, from the root עֹשֶׁר (enrichment), as per the words of the Sages, and Ashtaroth-karnayim were high mountains upon which were rocky strongholds; Scripture likens them to the horns of animals, just as it likens them to teeth, as in the matter stated (Job 39:28), \"upon a tooth of rock and stronghold\" (עַל שֶׁן סֶלַע וּמְצוּדָה), and likewise \"upon the horns of the altar\" (Exodus 29:12), and likewise <b>בְּקֶרֶן בֶּן שָׁמֶן</b> (Isaiah 5:1) — the Targum rendered \"a high mountain in a fat land.\" And there the he-goats and the goats and the ibexes and the like would ascend, the ashtaroth of the flocks, and therefore it is called Ashtaroth-karnayim. And likewise <b>to the Baal and to the Ashtaroth</b> (Judges 2:13) — the idolatrous image made in the shape of flocks, to protect them in their folly. And at the foot of the mountain they built a city whose name is Edrei, and this is what is stated (Deuteronomy 3:1), \"And Og king of Bashan went out, he and all his people, to battle at Edrei\" — for he gathered all his army to the city where the royal palace and the throne of the kingdom were.\n\nAnd the matter of the Sifrei (loc. cit.) — their midrash, the Rabbis of blessed memory said: \"who dwells in Ashtaroth in Edrei — if Og had not been harsh and dwelling in Ashtaroth, he would still have been harsh, for the province was harsh,\" etc. And therefore they interpret <b>in Ashtaroth</b> — \"for he was as harsh as Ashtaroth,\" meaning that his place of residence was like Ashtaroth-karnayim, the fortified city from which he and his family originated — for now he does not dwell in Ashtaroth, for he dwells in the land of the Amorite. And <b>the place of kingship</b> — meaning the province in which he dwelt, Edrei, for that is the name of the land of his kingdom, Bashan."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Rashi as combining two meanings of עַשְׁתָּרוֹת (flocks/wealth and crags/hardness); the phrase 'והרב מרכיב בו שתי לשונות' refers to Rashi grafting both senses together. The abbreviation בעה\"י is resolved as בעזרת השם, 'with HaShem's help.' The parenthetical reference '(להלן ב י ג יא)' likely points to two loci below: 2:10–11 and 3:11."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:44:12.141243707Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}